{% extends 'help/article.html.twig' %}

{% block body %}
    <p>
        Integrations live under Settings → Integrations. In every case <strong>you supply your own
        credentials</strong> — they are stored encrypted, used only for your company's requests,
        and never shared with another company on the platform. That is also why your drivers get
        email from your domain rather than ours.
    </p>

    <h2>Test before you rely on it</h2>
    <p>
        Every connection has a test button that makes a real call to the provider and reports what
        came back. It can test credentials you have typed but not yet saved. Use it every time:
        the alternative is discovering a bad API key when a driver says they never got the load.
    </p>

    <h2>ELD — Quantum, TrackPro or MAX</h2>
    <p>An ELD brings three things:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>A truck and driver roster</strong> you can import rather than type.</li>
        <li><strong>Live GPS positions</strong>, which feed the map and the proximity check in <a href="{{ path('help_article', { slug: 'assigning-drivers' }) }}">driver suggestions</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Odometer readings</strong>, which keep <a href="{{ path('help_article', { slug: 'maintenance' }) }}">mileage-based service intervals</a> honest.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        All three use the same API key field. MAX returns the richest data — it includes plate
        numbers, model years and driver contact details that the others do not expose.
    </p>

    <h3>Importing the roster</h3>
    <p>
        The import always previews first. It shows you what it will <strong>create</strong> (new
        records), <strong>link</strong> (provider records matched to ones you already have),
        <strong>refresh</strong> (existing records it will update) and <strong>retire</strong>
        (records the provider no longer reports). Nothing changes until you confirm.
    </p>
    <p>
        Read the link and retire lists on your first import. Linking is where a truck you typed in
        last week gets matched to the same truck from the ELD — and where you will spot it if the
        same unit exists twice.
    </p>

    <h3>One ELD at a time</h3>
    <p>
        A company can have exactly one ELD connected. Two providers both claiming the same truck
        is a data problem rather than a feature: you would have two sources of odometer and two
        sources of position, with no way to say which is right.
    </p>

    <h3>Switching provider</h3>
    <p>
        Because imported records are marked with the provider that created them, switching is a
        deliberate act. Before disconnecting, the app tells you exactly how much equipment is at
        stake — "14 trucks and 9 drivers" — and asks whether to release them. Release them and the
        new provider can adopt those records; keep them and they stay yours, unlinked.
    </p>

    <div class="warn">
        <p>Disconnecting without releasing leaves records nobody owns: the old provider is gone and
        the new one will create duplicates rather than adopt them. If you are changing ELD, release.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Fuel card — Mudflap</h2>
    <p>
        Connects with an access token, with a sandbox switch for testing. Purchases import
        automatically, roughly hourly, onto the
        <a href="{{ path('help_article', { slug: 'expenses' }) }}">expense ledger</a> with gallons,
        price per gallon and card savings broken out. A fleet can connect more than one fuel card —
        unlike ELDs, these do not conflict.
    </p>

    <h2>Google Maps</h2>
    <p>Three capabilities, each switched on independently, because they are billed separately:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Geocoding</strong> — turns stop addresses into coordinates. Everything else depends on this.</li>
        <li><strong>Places</strong> — address autocomplete as you type.</li>
        <li><strong>Routes</strong> — real road distance and drive time.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        With Routes off, mileage falls back to a straight-line estimate that runs well under true
        road miles, and the app labels it as an estimate. See
        <a href="{{ path('help_article', { slug: 'routing-and-miles' }) }}">Routing, miles and weather</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>SendGrid and Twilio</h2>
    <p>
        SendGrid needs an API key plus the from-address and from-name; Twilio needs the account
        SID, auth token and your from-number. Between them they are how a load reaches a driver —
        see <a href="{{ path('help_article', { slug: 'sending-loads' }) }}">Sending loads to drivers</a>.
        Either works alone.
    </p>

    <h2>Weather</h2>
    <p>
        Open-Meteo needs no account and no key — it is a straight on/off switch, and there is
        nothing to test. With it on, every stop with coordinates carries a four-day outlook and a
        reading for its appointment window.
    </p>
{% endblock %}
